# =============================================================================
# .trivyignore - explicit, time-boxed risk acceptances for the CardDemo images.
#
# Read this as a register, not as a mute button. Every entry names a finding for
# which NO published upstream image carries a fix, states where the vulnerable
# code sits, why it is not reachable from an attacker's position in this
# deployment, and what event retires the entry. Nothing that CAN be fixed appears
# here: the delivered application images (the nine Spring Boot services and the
# frontend) and the PostgreSQL image report ZERO fixable CRITICAL/HIGH findings
# without any suppression, and the one fixable application-layer advisory
# (netty-codec-dns GHSA-mfg7-5gfp-c4w3) was closed by raising the dependency in
# the root pom.xml rather than being listed here.
#
# Scanned to establish that no fix exists (Trivy 0.72.0, database of
# 2026-08-10):
#   grafana/grafana:13.1.2, 13.1.3 and :latest - identical residual findings
#   jaegertracing/jaeger:2.20.0 and :latest    - :latest IS 2.20.0
#
# Both remaining images are LOCAL OBSERVABILITY SIDECARS, not delivered
# application artefacts:
#   * docker-compose.yml publishes them on the host LOOPBACK only
#     (127.0.0.1:3001 for Grafana, 127.0.0.1:16686 for Jaeger); neither is
#     reachable from a routable interface and neither is fronted by the gateway.
#   * Their only inbound traffic on the private carddemo-net bridge is the OTLP
#     span stream the nine services export and Grafana's own scrape of Prometheus.
#     No end-user request, and no request carrying user-supplied data, reaches
#     either process.
#   * Neither holds business data. Jaeger's span storage is in-memory by design
#     and Grafana persists only dashboards and its own admin account.
#
# Full rationale, the CVE-by-CVE exposure analysis and the remediation trigger
# are recorded in docs/decision-log.md. Re-run
#   trivy image --scanners vuln --ignore-unfixed --severity CRITICAL,HIGH <image>
# WITHOUT this file to see the raw residual at any time.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Grafana server binary (usr/share/grafana/bin/grafana).
#
# Both findings are against the github.com/grafana/tempo revision Grafana vendors
# into its server binary for the Tempo datasource. CardDemo provisions the
# Prometheus and Jaeger datasources only (see the grafana_datasource config in
# docker-compose.yml), so no Tempo query path is ever constructed. Grafana
# publishes no release whose bin/grafana links a fixed revision.
#
# Retire when: an upstream Grafana release links tempo >= 2.10.3; then remove
# these two lines, rebuild observability/Dockerfile.grafana against it and rescan.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CVE-2026-21728 exp:2026-11-10
CVE-2026-28377 exp:2026-11-10

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jaeger all-in-one binary (cmd/jaeger/jaeger-linux).
#
# golang.org/x/text 0.38.0 (CVE-2026-56852) and google.golang.org/grpc 1.81.1
# (GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf) are compiled into the single-binary Jaeger v2 image.
# 2.20.0 is the newest published release AND is what :latest resolves to, so no
# tag or digest carries the fixed libraries. The gRPC surface is the OTLP receiver
# on the private bridge, spoken to only by the nine services' Micrometer/OTel
# exporters; the x/text path is language-tag parsing in the query UI, which is
# published on loopback only.
#
# Retire when: Jaeger publishes a release built with x/text >= 0.39.0 and
# grpc-go >= 1.82.1; then remove these two lines, re-pin the image digest in
# docker-compose.yml and k8s/deployment-jaeger.yaml, and rescan.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-11-10
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-11-10

# =============================================================================
# Kubernetes manifest checks (`trivy config k8s/`). Both entries below are
# accepted with reasons, not deferred; every other check the scan raises has been
# fixed in the manifests themselves.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KSV-0125 "Restrict container images to trusted registries" - 13 manifests.
#
# The check compares every image reference against an allow-list of public
# registries. CardDemo's nine services, the frontend, the PostgreSQL image and the
# Grafana image are built FROM THIS REPOSITORY by `docker compose build` and are
# referenced by their local names (carddemo/<service>:1.0.0), which no public
# registry allow-list can match. Substituting a registry host would name a
# location the repository does not publish to, so the reference would become
# wrong rather than safer. Every reference is either locally built or, for the
# upstream bases, digest-pinned.
#
# Retire when: the deployment gains a real registry; set CARDDEMO_IMAGE_REGISTRY
# to it, re-run the scan and drop this entry.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KSV-0125

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KSV-01010 "ConfigMap with sensitive content" - k8s/configmap.yaml.
#
# The finding names the keys SERVER_PORT and SPRING_DATA_REDIS_PORT, matched
# purely on the substring "PORT". Their values are the TCP port numbers 8080 and
# 6379. A port number is not a credential, and it must stay in the ConfigMap
# because every Deployment reads it with configMapKeyRef. Every actual secret in
# this deployment (database passwords, the Redis password, the PII key, the
# Grafana admin credential, the monitoring credential) is in k8s/secret.yaml and
# is injected from the environment - none is in a ConfigMap.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KSV-01010
